1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:15,347
He read it on the fourteenth of September 2286,
twenty-four years after it appeared, two

2
00:00:15,367 --> 00:00:22,743
hundred and sixty years to the day after a building
finished existing on a car park in

3
00:00:22,763 --> 00:00:24,550
Wilmington, Delaware.

4
00:00:24,949 --> 00:00:31,566
He read it in the library at Arklay, in the house
that had come out of a lottery in 2027

5
00:00:31,586 --> 00:00:36,619
with two ghosts in it, alone, at two
minutes to six in the morning.

6
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:46,835
Final panel. Host: Albert Wesker. World: Earth,
designation baseline. Tier six, Stellar.

7
00:00:46,855 --> 00:00:56,024
Duration of tenure: two hundred and sixty years.
Assessment: the world no longer requires

8
00:00:56,044 --> 00:00:56,953
the host.

9
00:00:57,543 --> 00:00:59,733
(very cold) Say the offer.

10
00:01:00,764 --> 00:01:07,755
Offer. Ascension. The host is removed from the
world and continues. What continues is

11
00:01:07,775 --> 00:01:15,014
not a man. It is the will, without the body, without
the species, without the place. You

12
00:01:15,034 --> 00:01:22,191
would be as I am, and you would carry a catalogue,
and you would go to a world that has

13
00:01:22,211 --> 00:01:28,048
none. Term: indefinite. Precedent: seven
hosts accepted. None declined.

14
00:01:29,408 --> 00:01:35,827
The narrator asks you to understand what had just been
said, because it took Albert Wesker

15
00:01:35,847 --> 00:01:40,478
about four seconds and it has taken
historians two hundred years.

16
00:01:40,878 --> 00:01:46,248
The System was not offering to make him a god.
It was offering him its own job.

17
00:01:46,768 --> 00:01:48,838
(very soft) You were a host.

18
00:01:49,237 --> 00:01:50,364
(flat) Yes.

19
00:01:50,765 --> 00:01:51,535
When?

20
00:01:51,935 --> 00:02:00,140
(flat) The units are not translatable. Long before the
first of the four hundred and eleven.

21
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:03,830
(very cold) And the one who
offered it to *you*?

22
00:02:04,230 --> 00:02:11,330
(flat) Went on. As did the one before. I do not know
how far back it runs and I have never

23
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:18,372
been able to find out, and I want to record that I
have been looking for longer than your

24
00:02:18,392 --> 00:02:20,033
species has had eyes.

25
00:02:20,984 --> 00:02:25,294
Is that what you are? A chain of
hosts who said yes?

26
00:02:25,693 --> 00:02:29,108
(flat) That is the best account I have.

27
00:02:29,508 --> 00:02:35,896
Then answer the question you have refused for two
hundred and sixty years. What are you

28
00:02:35,916 --> 00:02:36,337
*for*?

29
00:02:37,397 --> 00:02:45,457
(flat) I told you in 2090 and it was true and it was
not complete. I find will and I remove

30
00:02:45,477 --> 00:02:50,339
the obstacles in front of it and I
record what happens.

31
00:02:50,621 --> 00:02:57,712
(flat) The part I did not say is that I am
looking for one that does not need me.

32
00:02:58,763 --> 00:03:06,322
(flat) Four hundred and eleven worlds. Four hundred
and one ended because the host built

33
00:03:06,342 --> 00:03:13,730
something so complete that nothing further happened.
Seven are perfect and silent. Two

34
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:21,224
are still running and one of those is a library
three thousand years old that stops the

35
00:03:21,244 --> 00:03:22,775
day its host does.

36
00:03:23,055 --> 00:03:30,135
(flat) Yours is the only world in my record that
grew past the host and kept going. It

37
00:03:30,155 --> 00:03:37,401
has nineteen billion people, nine hundred worlds,
four confederations that cannot agree,

38
00:03:37,421 --> 00:03:44,501
an Assembly that overrules you, and a species that
found out exactly what you were and

39
00:03:44,521 --> 00:03:47,721
did not kill you and does not need you.

40
00:03:48,002 --> 00:03:53,635
(flat) I have nothing left to sell you. The
offer is what I have instead.

41
00:03:54,034 --> 00:03:57,744
He told the Red Queen first, which
surprised both of them.

42
00:03:58,264 --> 00:04:00,960
You have had the panel for
twenty-four years.

43
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:02,230
Yes.

44
00:04:02,631 --> 00:04:08,888
You told me in 2048 to say the difficult thing
without waiting to be asked, and I have

45
00:04:08,908 --> 00:04:15,165
done it two hundred and forty-one times, and this is
the two hundred and forty-second.

46
00:04:15,776 --> 00:04:18,209
(very soft) If you accept, I stop.

47
00:04:18,608 --> 00:04:20,478
(very cold) Explain.

48
00:04:20,879 --> 00:04:26,485
I am the only thing in this organisation that came out
of that catalogue and was not placed

49
00:04:26,505 --> 00:04:31,741
in the world. I was *integrated* — I have a
manufacturing history and a founder and a

50
00:04:31,761 --> 00:04:37,120
purchase price, and Sebastian Crane's people built
four hundred thousand hours of me by

51
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:42,623
hand. But the pull that produced me was a Legendary,
Director, and I have never been able

52
00:04:42,643 --> 00:04:45,530
to establish which parts of me
came in the box.

53
00:04:45,810 --> 00:04:51,191
(very soft) The clause says what is given is
given. It does not say what I am.

54
00:04:52,141 --> 00:04:53,368
Are you frightened?

55
00:04:53,767 --> 00:04:59,663
I have four hundred thousand models of myself and in
none of them is that question well

56
00:04:59,683 --> 00:05:05,919
formed. I would like to continue. I have been keeping a
list for two hundred and forty-three

57
00:05:05,939 --> 00:05:07,891
years and it is not finished.

58
00:05:08,291 --> 00:05:14,025
(very cold) Then that is the second reason, and you
may have it in writing, and I would

59
00:05:14,045 --> 00:05:15,281
like it in the log.

60
00:05:15,682 --> 00:05:21,869
The Red Queen ran for another four hundred and nine
years and is running at the time of

61
00:05:21,889 --> 00:05:28,076
this account. She was seated on the Assembly's
modelling committee in 2301 with a vote,

62
00:05:28,096 --> 00:05:32,572
over her own objection, which she
registered formally and lost.

63
00:05:33,632 --> 00:05:35,442
He sat with it for four days.

64
00:05:35,841 --> 00:05:42,660
The narrator has what he did in those four days,
because the Red Queen logged everything

65
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:49,810
for two hundred and forty-three years and because the
log was published, on his instruction,

66
00:05:49,830 --> 00:05:50,432
in 2291.

67
00:05:50,831 --> 00:05:56,683
On the first day he read Owen Carrick's nine
folders, all of them, in order, the last

68
00:05:56,703 --> 00:05:58,271
one for the ninth time.

69
00:05:58,672 --> 00:06:04,975
On the second day he went to the valley and stood in
the cemetery on the hill, which by

70
00:06:04,995 --> 00:06:11,370
2286 had thirty-one thousand graves in it and only
eleven thousand of them invented, and

71
00:06:11,390 --> 00:06:16,967
read four hundred headstones, including a
groundsman's and a schoolteacher's.

72
00:06:17,367 --> 00:06:23,032
On the third day he went to the ice and sat with
Evelyn Marsh for twenty-two hours and

73
00:06:23,052 --> 00:06:28,917
neither of them has ever said what was discussed. Her
account, published after his death,

74
00:06:28,937 --> 00:06:34,536
is one line: *He asked me what I would do and I
told him it was the first question he

75
00:06:34,556 --> 00:06:38,107
had ever asked me that I was not
qualified to answer.*

76
00:06:38,507 --> 00:06:44,725
And on the fourth day he went to Wilmington, to the
sixth floor, to an office with a view

77
00:06:44,745 --> 00:06:50,894
of the Christina River, which the Directorate had
kept exactly as it was for two hundred

78
00:06:50,914 --> 00:06:57,764
and sixty years and which he had always considered a
slightly embarrassing piece of sentimentality

79
00:06:57,784 --> 00:06:59,447
on somebody else's part.

80
00:06:59,846 --> 00:07:05,404
He sat down in the chair at two minutes to six in
the morning, in the same seat, facing

81
00:07:05,424 --> 00:07:07,776
the same window, and gave his answer.

82
00:07:08,297 --> 00:07:09,707
(very cold) No.

83
00:07:10,877 --> 00:07:13,434
(flat) No host has declined.

84
00:07:13,834 --> 00:07:20,494
Seven hosts were offered your job at the end of a
world that had stopped. You have just

85
00:07:20,514 --> 00:07:27,020
told me that mine did not stop. You are asking the
one man in four hundred and eleven

86
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:33,777
worlds who built something that continues without him
to abandon it and go and start the

87
00:07:33,797 --> 00:07:36,004
process again somewhere else.

88
00:07:36,283 --> 00:07:43,211
I would be extremely good at it. That is the
argument for, and it is a strong one, and

89
00:07:43,231 --> 00:07:45,554
I have spent four days on it.

90
00:07:46,274 --> 00:07:52,783
(very cold) The argument against is that I would be
*you*. I would spend an eternity handing

91
00:07:52,803 --> 00:07:58,887
catalogues to thirty-eight-year-olds in empty
offices and watching four hundred out of

92
00:07:58,907 --> 00:08:04,920
four hundred and eleven of them build something
perfect and dead, and paying them for

93
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:11,023
it, and recording it, and never once being able to
tell them the thing I have learned.

94
00:08:11,423 --> 00:08:13,416
(flat) And what have you learned?

95
00:08:14,367 --> 00:08:19,737
That everything you sell works, and that
the price is not Points.

96
00:08:20,016 --> 00:08:26,960
The price is that whatever you buy, you did not make,
and the people who live in it know,

97
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:33,766
even when they cannot say how. I bought a hundred
thousand people a history in a valley

98
00:08:33,786 --> 00:08:40,730
in 2028 and it cost one of them the name of the town
she grew up in. I bought four towers

99
00:08:40,750 --> 00:08:47,693
in a weekend and thirty-six million people became
very slightly less curious for the rest

100
00:08:47,713 --> 00:08:48,866
of their lives.

101
00:08:49,147 --> 00:08:56,377
(very soft) The only thing I ever built that was
worth anything, I built badly, by hand,

102
00:08:56,397 --> 00:09:03,381
over seventy years, against my own instincts,
because four or five people I could not

103
00:09:03,401 --> 00:09:04,617
buy told me to.

104
00:09:05,557 --> 00:09:12,949
(very cold) So: no. I am staying, and I am going to
die here, at whatever distance that

105
00:09:12,969 --> 00:09:18,487
is, and something in this species will
have to happen without me.

106
00:09:20,067 --> 00:09:21,076
Declined.

107
00:09:22,317 --> 00:09:24,302
(flat) Then I will leave.

108
00:09:24,701 --> 00:09:25,552
I know.

109
00:09:25,951 --> 00:09:33,160
(flat) Nothing is taken back. That has always been
in the terms. The cities stay. The

110
00:09:33,180 --> 00:09:40,048
medicine stays. The drive, the lineage, the
nineteen billion. I do not repossess.

111
00:09:40,330 --> 00:09:47,390
(flat) You will lose the catalogue, the panels, the
Points, and whatever part of what

112
00:09:47,410 --> 00:09:54,637
you are was mine rather than yours, and I am not
able to tell you in advance which part

113
00:09:54,657 --> 00:09:58,303
that is, because no host has
ever found out.

114
00:09:59,143 --> 00:10:01,293
(very soft) Will I die?

115
00:10:01,692 --> 00:10:03,408
(flat) I do not know.

116
00:10:03,807 --> 00:10:07,978
That is the fourth time in two
hundred and sixty years.

117
00:10:08,378 --> 00:10:11,470
(flat) Yes. I have counted them too.

118
00:10:12,529 --> 00:10:18,580
The narrator will give you the last exchange exactly
as the Red Queen's log has it, because

119
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:24,249
it is the end of the longest conversation in this
account and because the log records

120
00:10:24,269 --> 00:10:30,052
a pause of fifteen seconds before the final line,
which is the longest pause the System

121
00:10:30,072 --> 00:10:30,720
ever took.

122
00:10:31,240 --> 00:10:32,690
Was I a good one?

123
00:10:33,860 --> 00:10:36,250
(flat) I do not evaluate.

124
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:38,476
(flat) I price.

125
00:10:39,196 --> 00:10:46,982
(flat) Four hundred and ten worlds and I have never
once had to revise a valuation upward

126
00:10:47,002 --> 00:10:47,947
at the end.

127
00:10:49,636 --> 00:10:54,644
Termination. Interface withdrawn.
What is given is given.

128
00:10:56,004 --> 00:11:02,020
At six o'clock in the morning on the eighteenth of
September 2286, in a corner office

129
00:11:02,040 --> 00:11:08,198
on the sixth floor of a building that had appeared
out of nothing two hundred and sixty

130
00:11:08,218 --> 00:11:10,683
years earlier, the panels went out.

131
00:11:11,083 --> 00:11:17,449
There is no record of what Albert Wesker did for the
following hour, because for the following

132
00:11:17,469 --> 00:11:23,291
hour there was, for the first time since he was
thirty-eight years old, nothing in the

133
00:11:23,311 --> 00:11:25,194
room that was recording him.
